>> B2: When a frame is required but none is free, some frame must be
>> evicted.  Describe your code for choosing a frame to evict.

When I choose a victim, I used the Clock Algorithm. It means that
I set accessed bit of the pages to zero while I'm searching for a victim.
Therefore, after I check the whole lists, the first one must be selected
as a victim since it is not accessed currently.

>> B3: When a process P obtains a frame that was previously used by a
>> process Q, how do you adjust the page table (and any other data
>> structures) to reflect the frame Q no longer has?

When I get the page from the Q, I could know the page is from the Q
by its struct member. Then, I could get the thread structure of the Q
and clear the page from the Q by using pagedir_clear_page.

>> B4: Explain your heuristic for deciding whether a page fault for an
>> invalid virtual address should cause the stack to be extended into
>> the page that faulted.

I created verify_stack() function that verifies whether the fault address
is valid to cause the stack to be expanded or not. And, I created expand_stack()
function that expands stack. Also, I modified check_address function into that
can verify addresses and expand the stack.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B5: Explain the basics of your VM synchronization design. In
>> particular, explain how it prevents deadlock. (Refer to the
>> textbook for an explanation of the necessary conditions for
>> deadlock.)

I defined two locks for swap and page structure.

The interface functions for swap and pages acquires corresponding
lock inside the function and the functions call by them does not
acquires the same lock.

Also, there's no case that needs both of them at the same time
since I do not used both lock at the same function, so there's no
circular wait.

>> B6: A page fault in process P can cause another process Q's frame
>> to be evicted.  How do you ensure that Q cannot access or modify
>> the page during the eviction process?  How do you avoid a race
>> between P evicting Q's frame and Q faulting the page back in?

I used lru_lock when allocating and evicting the page. Since, Q need
to get lock to get page back, Q need to wait until evicting is
finished.

>> B7: Suppose a page fault in process P causes a page to be read from
>> the file system or swap.  How do you ensure that a second process Q
>> cannot interfere by e.g. attempting to evict the frame while it is
>> still being read in?

There's a pinned flag which prevent such situation. If pinned flag is
set to 1, no process cannot evict the page. I set the pin to 1 while
reading, writing, and handling the page fault and set it to 0 after it
finishes. Therefore, Q cannot interfere P until P finishes it's operation.

>> B8: Explain how you handle access to paged-out pages that occur
>> during system calls.  Do you use page faults to bring in pages (as
>> in user programs), or do you have a mechanism for "locking" frames
>> into physical memory, or do you use some other design?  How do you
>> gracefully handle attempted accesses to invalid virtual addresses?

I used page faults to bring in pages. And before call system calls,
I called check_address() functions first to check whether the address
is invalid or not.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: A single lock for the whole VM system would make
>> synchronization easy, but limit parallelism.  On the other hand,
>> using many locks complicates synchronization and raises the
>> possibility for deadlock but allows for high parallelism.  Explain
>> where your design falls along this continuum and why you chose to
>> design it this way.

Since locking the whole VM system limits the parallism so that leads
to the poor performance, I used multiple locks, however, each lock is
only used for the corresponding functions to avoid deadlock.

